# A selector list names the whole module, like every other import

`import credential.{credentialEnv, credentialFile}` loads `std/credential.ssc` and every
definition in it — exactly what `import credential.credentialEnv` and `[credentialEnv](std/credential.ssc)`
load. The names between the braces are NOT read.

That is not a shortcut, it is the only thing the spelling can mean here: a selector list is a
statement about NAMESPACING, and Tier 0 has none. So the list is normalised to `.*`
(`Source.selectorsToStar`) and the module arrives whole.

**This fixture used to assert the opposite** — it had no `.expected`, which is how this gate says
"must be refused", and its prose argued that accepting the spelling would mean accepting a promise
the language cannot keep. The premise was right and the conclusion was wrong: refusing did not keep
the promise either, it just cost three `std/mapreduce/` modules and made v3's own front disagree
with uniml, which dropped the line and ran. The owner's decision of 2026-08-14 is that it works.

The proof it is the SAME load is that this prints what `scala-import.expected` prints, from the
same module, through the other spelling.

```scalascript
import credential.{credentialEnv, credentialFile}

val c = credentialEnv("API_TOKEN")
println(c.kind)
println(c.source)
println(c.scheme)
```
